<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thietmar of Merseburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">975–<a href="/years/1018">1018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thietmar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> (975–1018) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noble who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Merseburg in 1009. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Chronicon, covering the Ottonian kings and emperors up to 1018, is a principal source for eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a>. It includes a well-known description of the Lutician federation and its pagan temple at Riedegost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thietmar-of-merseburg.jpg" alt="Thietmar of Merseburg" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harald Rossa · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tafel_1009_Bischof_Thietmar_v._Merseburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
